Deterministic machines, for rollback netplay: two players' machines must stay identical, and a
machine that loads another's state must go on exactly as the one that saved it.

- running_machine: the base time the clock chips start from is fixed (1998-01-01 00:00:00 UTC)
  instead of the host's clock, and system_time's "local" time is UTC (localtime follows the
  browser's time zone). Tekken 3 reads an RTC4543.
- device_rtc_interface: the clock's registers go in the save state. Chips rebuild their
  registers from them every tick, so a machine that loaded a state went back to its own time.
- PSX root counters and SIO: their post-load re-armed their timers from the current count (whole
  counter ticks) or from now, moving them; the timers are in the save state as they were.
- screen: the fork's shortcut in configure() (skip when nothing changed, after 10 calls) counts
  calls outside the save state, so it behaved differently on a machine that loaded a state.
- sound: the fork's periodic sound update ran at retro_fps^2 Hz, retro_fps being host state
  (each machine's own history of screen modes); its timer splits the CPUs' time slices, so now a
  fixed 3600 Hz (the same 60 chunks per 60 Hz frame).

